{:activity/_scope [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060681} :activity/id #uuid "84d7f054-fed4-524c-b241-f09c7fd42e5e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060940 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can apply some operations to the augmented matrix that do not change the set of solutions for the corresponding system. These operations are called **elementary row operations**. \n\nAfter applying an **elementary row operation** to the augmented matrix, the new corresponding system will have the same solutions.\n\nThere are three types of elementary row operations:\n\n- switching any two rows: \n\n$$\nR_i \\leftrightarrow R_j\n$$\n\n- multiplying (each element of) a row by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nR_i := k R_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- adding a multiple of one row to another row: \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j+k R_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\nIn the notations above, the \"$:=$\" symbol is called the **assignment operator.** We use this to assign new values to every element of a given row.\n\nFor example, the row operation $R_1 := 2 R_1$ means, \"create a new row $1$ by taking the old row $1$ and multiplying it by $2.$\" When we do this, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}]\\,{R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-1) & 2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA few final points on notation.\n\n- We use the similarity symbol ($\\sim$) to indicate when one matrix is transformed into another matrix using elementary row operations.\n\n- The assignment operator $:=$ is sometimes called the \"walrus operator\" because it looks like a walrus on its side!" :tutorial/id #uuid "7aa753a7-1450-53dd-8e61-71e07952cecb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3150 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3df97c2e-febb-5f72-be71-e1d739dbbd92" :step/math-academy-id 17183 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060682}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059580 :question/id #uuid "836f2f1b-5dd4-5dba-81cc-eb9ffe7db8d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4693" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/62/627d12115d61ddfdedf9db08330269de2137a23abceada977d039e90e5b88675.png)\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2 := R_2 +(- 4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1$ means that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4+(-4)(1) & 2+(-4)(2) & -1+(-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1afe82-86ba-5a15-9037-74717692ecb2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050163}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5a9bba7-f581-5372-a873-0fc17a55fb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & -9 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056126} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c177a1-6771-55e3-bd09-bf47def49ac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 9 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056127} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1d8e7e-44fc-5700-9bdc-f19ade4437e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056128} {:answer/id #uuid "5f95897b-76c9-50f9-ba67-9a60ae42a4ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056129} {:answer/id #uuid "64489d72-05e0-5199-9ab5-2517bee16eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056130}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fe11ff0e-55ca-57c1-896c-442215af5968" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056125} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e95e2e5-f4c7-556f-9d3b-6806aa203aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386022} {:answer/id #uuid "93d2127b-e5d6-5f6a-b77b-e49e6b3917a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386023} {:answer/id #uuid "dfcd1624-4c95-5d1d-b7c3-eadfb5affb1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & -9 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386024} {:answer/id #uuid "84d65aa0-7f3f-56d8-8813-60439890bf5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386025} {:answer/id #uuid "f47ccd2f-8569-5e0e-ae2f-f78a4ee09a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 9 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386022} :answer-field/id #uuid "6265e561-15e0-5e23-846a-8eab1f2ab8dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "73199f2e-4821-5c88-b3d1-fc53d8677008" :question/math-academy-id "q-10248" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $M,$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}$ on $M?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $3$ times the first row to the third row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3+(3)(-1) & -1+(3)(2) & 9+(3)(4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 21\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03dc59cb-fd09-565f-9b4f-7c5b09fa0d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235821} {:answer/id #uuid "13851fd1-8586-537b-b24a-6845ca174846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235931} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca00ef3-9d64-5399-b711-41bbe0eaeaea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236097} {:answer/id #uuid "7c169d8a-0156-5a46-bcad-6fcf3b499bde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236593} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f87e96-eee1-5c3b-b11d-b31db823cd13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236593} :answer-field/id #uuid "1923a0a4-f52b-5133-bedc-c31b8ed88e76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "855eebc5-e47a-5262-b735-bfa0d3ece889" :question/math-academy-id "q-10250" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237897 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b0ae7c7-831d-5433-9390-889330306d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235871} {:answer/id #uuid "30eaaa83-5cd1-5e22-a46e-6e3b5195a079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236128} {:answer/id #uuid "bab3efd1-d5b4-58ad-bfff-d99746961975"